  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 Days...\n\nAn upper level trough is currently centered over southern ID and \nis slowly moving eastwards. This feature is our primary weather \nmaker for the next few days. Showers and thunderstorms already \nmade their presence known last evening with rainfall rates under \nthe strongest cores exceeding 2\" /hr. This will be the theme over \nthe next day or so.\n\nThe low pressure system is vertically stacked for the most part \ndue to the same limited upper level winds that are making it move \nas slow as it is. This is aiding in the limited shear across the \nregion with the exception of directly in the low itself. This \nlimited shear is the primary reason that there is minimal concern \nfor any organized severe weather over the next 2 days. The one \npossible exception to this, is as the low approaches the Bighorns\nthis afternoon, some CAMs show a short lived complex of stronger \nstorms. This is believable as the synoptic lifting mechanism \ncombines with the orographic one, the minimal (500 J/Kg) CAPE \nshould be sufficient for stronger storms to roll off the Bighorns.\nDue to the quite weak low level shear though, these storms should\nnot last long. With the vertically stacked vorticity and slow \nmoving cold core low, I would not be shocked to see a few cold air\nfunnels during the day today though. \n\nThe main story with this event will be the large amounts of \nrainfall. PWAT values could exceed 1.25\" over much of the CWA \ntoday as the easterly surface winds ahead of the low advect in all\nof the moisture out of the Great Plains. Completely saturated \nsoundings depicted by nearly all models are a rare occurrence in \nthis climate; these are indicative of efficient rainfall \nproducing storms. As mentioned above, we have already seen very \nheavy rainfall rates, and the likelihood of this continuing under\nthe strongest cores is quite high. Even locations that do not \nhave any convection move overhead will still see steady stratiform\nrainfall through the day today which will easily total at least a\nhalf inch for the entire forecast area. Actually, the NBM 10th \npercentile shows over 1.5\" for a corridor from Big Timber to \nHysham, including Billings. This would suggest that there is at \nleast a 90% chance that we see that much rainfall. While the NBM \n90th percentile values are a bit high, localized pockets of 4+ \ninches should be expected in association with the convective \ncores. Flash flooding, esepcailly in locations prone to such, is a\nlarge concern over the next 24 hours as some locations could pick\nup the equivalent of 25% of their average annual rainfall in just\na few hours. \n\nIn terms of mountain snow, confidence is increasing that snow \nlevels remain at or above 10,0000 ft for the duration of the \nevent. This could bring a light accumulation to the peak of the \nBeartooth Pass Sunday AM, in addition to mixed precip throughout \nthe event. The highest peaks of the mountains (ie. Granite Peak) \ncould pick up a total of 3-6\" of snowfall through the entire \nevent.\n\nSunday we will be behind the low pressure system, however wrap\naround precipitation is still possible. Scattered showers across \nthe forecast area will linger through the day due to a TROWAL like\nfeature but will have less moisture left to work with. Impacts \nwith this rain on Sunday should be confined to locations that \nalready picked up a lot of rainfall Today, meaning that widespread\nimpacts are not expected.\n\nLooking ahead to the work week, we should see much drier\nconditions. Behind the weekend low a dirty ridge builds in across\nthe area. This will allow for temperatures to slowly rebound\nthrough the week to near average. There will be a stream of\nmonsoonal moisture that is originating in Tropical Pacific\nreaching into western parts of the CWA. This could bring isolated\nshowers and storms for much of the week, but nowhere close to what\nwe are seeing this weekend. As of right now the chance for precip\nfor locations west of Billings is 30% with even lower numbers \nelsewhere. WMR",
